## Proof

We are given $U(t) = e^{-t} B(e^{2t})$, where $B(t)$ is a standard Brownian motion for $t \geq 0$.

**Mean.** Since $B$ is a standard Brownian motion, $\mathbb{E}[B(u)] = 0$ for all $u \geq 0$. Hence
$$\mathbb{E}[U(t)] = e^{-t}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[B(e^{2t})\right] = 0.$$

**Covariance.** For $s, t \geq 0$,
$$\operatorname{Cov}(U(s), U(t)) = \mathbb{E}[U(s)U(t)] = e^{-s}e^{-t}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[B(e^{2s})\,B(e^{2t})\right].$$

Using the fundamental covariance property of standard Brownian motion,
$$\mathbb{E}[B(a)B(b)] = \min(a, b), \qquad a, b \geq 0,$$
we obtain
$$\operatorname{Cov}(U(s), U(t)) = e^{-(s+t)}\min\!\left(e^{2s}, e^{2t}\right).$$

**Simplification.** Without loss of generality assume $s \leq t$. Then $e^{2s} \leq e^{2t}$, so $\min(e^{2s}, e^{2t}) = e^{2s}$, and
$$\operatorname{Cov}(U(s), U(t)) = e^{-(s+t)}\,e^{2s} = e^{2s - s - t} = e^{s - t} = e^{-(t-s)}.$$

By symmetry in $s$ and $t$, the general expression is
$$\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(U(s), U(t)) = e^{-|t-s|}, \qquad s, t \geq 0.}$$

**Verification of stationarity.** Note that $\operatorname{Var}(U(t)) = e^{-|t-t|} = e^{0} = 1$ for all $t$, and the covariance depends only on the lag $|t-s|$. Thus $U(t)$ is a (wide-sense) stationary Gaussian process — in fact the stationary Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process — with covariance function $e^{-|t-s|}$.

